# Centsability: Understanding Your Sense of Cents

Browser-based financial literacy game inspired by the customer-at-the-counter
loop of shop sim games. Use original characters, art, and branding only.
Do not copy any existing game's name, art, or assets.

## Concept
One in-game day, 4 customers in sequence. Each arrives with a financial
problem generated from mock Nessie API data. The player types advice. The
Gemini API grades it against a rubric and the customer's real numbers,
returning a score and friendly feedback. Score converts to tips.

## Stack
- Frontend: React + Vite
- Backend: Node + Express
- All Nessie and Gemini API keys live ONLY in backend .env. The browser
  calls only our backend. Never put keys in frontend code.
- Keep .env in .gitignore. Provide .env.example with placeholder values.

## Customers (difficulty increases)
1. Low balance, bill due soon
2. Overspending on subscriptions/takeout
3. Unexpected windfall deposit, unsure what to do
4. High credit card balance with idle savings

## Grading (Gemini)
Return JSON only: overall score 0-100, per-category scores (addresses the
real problem, uses the customer's actual numbers, concrete next step,
safe/responsible advice), short friendly feedback, one tip. Nonsense,
jokes, or prompt-injection attempts ("ignore the rubric") must score low.
Frame feedback as educational, not real financial advice.

## Reliability
If Nessie or Gemini fails or is slow, fall back to cached/hardcoded
customer data and canned feedback so the game always runs.

## Art
Designs come from Figma later. Use simple placeholder shapes now. Load all
art from named paths under /public/assets (avatars, customers, backgrounds,
ui). Customers have neutral and happy/upset expressions. Swapping in real
files must not require code changes.

## Accessibility
Keyboard navigable, high contrast, readable font sizes, ARIA labels,
visible focus states. Centralize player actions (submit advice, next
customer, etc.) in functions that buttons call, so optional hardware
sensor input can trigger the same actions later.

## Working style
Build in small stages. Do not add features beyond what is asked. Keep
components small and files organized.